    "id": "news-1793445951419-0",
    "title": "Uganda Begins Troop Withdrawal from Somalia After 20 Years",
    "original_title": "Uganda begins troop pullout from Somalia after 20 years",
    "slug": "uganda-begins-troop-withdrawal-from-somalia-after-20-years",
    "summary": "Uganda has started pulling its troops out of Somalia after two decades of fighting Al-Shabaab. The first 109 soldiers left Mogadishu on Friday, following an announcement by Uganda's army chief.",
    "content": "Uganda has commenced the withdrawal of its military forces from Somalia, marking the end of a 20-year deployment aimed at combating the militant group Al-Shabaab. On Friday, a Kenya-registered aircraft chartered by Uganda departed from Mogadishu's airport carrying 109 Ugandan soldiers—the first group to leave since the withdrawal was announced. The decision was made public on Thursday by Uganda's army chief, General Muhoozi Kainerugaba.\n\nThe withdrawal follows a request from Ugandan President Yoweri Museveni to Somali President Hassan Sheikh Mohamud, indicating that a portion of Uganda's forces would be withdrawn before November 15. President Mohamud had reportedly asked for a delay in the drawdown, but Museveni rebuffed the plea. Uganda is a key contributor to the African Union Transition Mission in Somalia (ATMIS), which supports Somali security forces against Al-Shabaab.\n\nThe Ugandan contingent has been part of the AMISOM/ATMIS mission since 2007, playing a significant role in pushing Al-Shabaab out of Mogadishu and other major cities. However, the group remains a potent threat, carrying out frequent attacks. The withdrawal comes amid ongoing discussions about the future of international military support in Somalia and the capacity of Somali forces to assume full responsibility for security.\n\nFor the Somali diaspora, the withdrawal raises concerns about the stability of the country and the potential for increased insecurity. Many in the diaspora have family members in Somalia and are invested in the nation's peace process. The drawdown could affect the momentum against Al-Shabaab and potentially lead to a security vacuum if not carefully managed. Diaspora communities often advocate for continued international support and a smooth transition to Somali-led security.\n\nThe departure of Ugandan troops is part of a broader plan to gradually reduce the number of foreign forces in Somalia, with ATMIS expected to hand over security responsibilities to the Somali National Army by the end of 2024. The international community has emphasized the need for a conditions-based withdrawal to prevent gains made against Al-Shabaab from being reversed. The success of this transition is critical for Somalia's long-term stability and for the diaspora's hopes of a peaceful homeland.\n\nRead the original report from Somalia Guardian here: https://www.somaliguardian.com/news/somalia-news/uganda-begins-troop-pullout-from-somalia/",
